---
// Lattice comparison page — a standalone Astro route (NOT a Starlight page), so
// it renders full-bleed with the engine's own tokens, a sibling to index.astro.
//
// This page LEADS WITH THE ARGUMENT, then compares. Front half is the manifesto
// (problem → what we believe → on speed → what we commit to), drawn from the
// same convictions as principles.md and story.md. Back half is the HONEST, FAIR,
// OPINIONATED comparison: it credits each rival's real strengths, makes the
// deterministic/craft case with sourced evidence, and concedes Lattice's own
// weaknesses. The cited source-of-truth for the field survey is
// engineering/decisions/2026-06-14-competitive-analysis.md; the July-2026 receipt
// refresh (KPMG, Deloitte, Gamma sizing, Copilot adoption, ISO/IEC 42001, the
// "slop" cultural signal) lives in the PR that repositioned this page. Keep the
// load-bearing numbers in sync with those and re-verify before editing.
//
// Static content only — rendered to HTML server-side (no client: directive →
// zero JS). The page shell (head, topbar, footer, nav toggle) mirrors index.astro.
import '../styles/landing.css';
import SiteHeader from '../components/site/SiteHeader.astro';
import ResourceHints from '../components/site/ResourceHints.astro';
import EngineWarm from '../components/site/EngineWarm.astro';
import { SectionHead } from '../components/landing/sections.tsx';
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { join } from 'node:path';
import { joinBase } from '../lib/base-url.mjs';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../lib/nav.mjs';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const url = (p) => joinBase(base, p);

// Palette list = the distinct names baked into the generated token CSS — one
// source of truth, same as the landing topbar selector.
const docsRoot = process.cwd();
const tokensCss = readFileSync(join(docsRoot, 'src/styles/lattice-tokens.generated.css'), 'utf8');
const palettes = [...new Set([...tokensCss.matchAll(/data-palette="([^"]+)"/g)].map((m) => m[1]))];

const SOURCE_DOC = `${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/engineering/decisions/2026-06-14-competitive-analysis.md`;

// ── What we commit to (the policy section) ──────────────────────────────────
const COMMITMENTS = [
	{
		head: 'Deterministic by contract',
		body: 'The same Markdown and the same theme render the same pixels — on your laptop, a colleague’s, a build server, this year and next. In regulated work that’s not a nicety: reproducibility and auditability are what standards like ISO/IEC 42001 now ask for. It’s also just how you put your name on a deck without re-reading every slide. The version you approved is the version that opens in the room.',
	},
	{
		head: 'The AI writes; the engine renders',
		body: 'A model can draft the words. It never touches the pixels. Probabilistic reasoning, reproducible output — the way regulated work already keeps its reasoning layer and its record layer apart. AI generators fuse content, facts, and design into one roll of the dice and get each one only mostly right.',
	},
	{
		head: 'Your model, your account, your spend',
		body: 'Drive any of 500+ models through your own OpenRouter account, switch whenever you like, and watch the meter the whole time. We don’t own the AI layer — you do. The tool that generates your deck should not also be the tool that bills you for it and swaps the model out from under you.',
	},
	{
		head: 'Your work is yours to keep',
		body: 'Lattice is open source under AGPL-3.0, and a Lattice Output Exception frees every deck you make — no license attaches to your slides, ever. It runs offline, phones home to nobody, and can’t be switched off under you. Leave whenever you like: every deck is still a plain PDF sitting next to the Markdown that built it.',
	},
];

// ── The four families ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Credit each family's real strengths; name where each breaks once the deck has
// to answer to a board. Gamma's scale (verified: $2.1B, ~70M users, Nov 2025)
// is stated plainly — we do not pretend the rivals are weak.
const FAMILIES = [
	{
		name: 'AI generators',
		tools: 'Gamma · Beautiful.ai · Decktopus · Presentations.ai · Plus AI · (Tome, RIP)',
		win: 'They are fast to a first draft, and the category is real money — Gamma alone reached a $2.1B valuation and roughly 70 million users. A prompt becomes a presentable draft in about a minute, and it looks modern out of the box. Nothing kills a blank page quicker. For a throwaway internal deck, grab one.',
		fall: 'The draft is where the work starts, not ends. They roll the dice every time — regenerate one slide and the layout reshuffles under you. Exports break: Gamma flattens its PowerPoint to uneditable images and swaps your fonts for Calibri. Every deck drifts toward the same house style, and the numbers arrive with no sources.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Office suites + AI',
		tools: 'PowerPoint + Copilot · Google Slides + Gemini · Apple Keynote',
		win: 'These own the boardroom today. PowerPoint is the universal format, Google runs the best live co-editing on the market, and everyone already knows how to drive all three. That reach is a real moat, and it isn’t going anywhere.',
		fall: 'The file is a binary blob or a cloud object, so you can’t reliably diff two versions — you get final_v3_FINAL.pptx instead. Fonts fall back and layouts reflow on the next machine, so “looks right here” means nothing. The brand guide sits in a folder nobody enforces. And Copilot rolls the dice like the rest — even bundled into Office, it’s paid for by only about 3% of seats.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Code engines',
		tools: 'Marp · reveal.js · Slidev · Beamer · Quarto · Spectacle',
		win: 'Our own family. Free, open source, plain text, deterministic, versionable. If you live in a terminal, these are the lock-in-free choice, and Slidev is the sharpest of the lot.',
		fall: 'None of them ships a design system. You get a handful of themes and raw CSS, then you build every layout yourself — and boardroom polish takes real design work most people can’t spare. Several use a browser-print PDF path, so the output changes machine to machine. They’re for developers; we’re for anyone who has to make the deck.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Design & collab tools',
		tools: 'Pitch · Canva · Figma Slides',
		win: 'Strong on design, strong on collaboration. A marketing team that wants beautiful slides and live multiplayer editing gets both here. Figma’s design control and Canva’s template range are the real thing.',
		fall: 'The deck lives in someone else’s cloud as a proprietary object. You can’t rebuild it from text, and you steer the brand through a GUI kit instead of a system. Canva output reads as “a Canva template.” Figma Slides still ships PPTX export bugs. Pitch cut roughly two-thirds of its staff in 2024 and pulled back from its hyper-growth bet.',
	},
];

// ── The comparison matrix (capability, not feature count) ────────────────────
// Marks: 'yes' (strong) · 'part' (partial/conditional) · 'no'. Lattice does NOT
// win every row — the ❌s are the point; they make the ✅s credible.
const COLS = ['Lattice', 'AI generators', 'Office + AI', 'Code engines', 'Design / collab'];
const ROWS = [
	{
		axis: 'Deterministic, reproducible output',
		why: 'Same input → identical pixels, every time.',
		marks: ['yes', 'no', 'part', 'yes', 'no'],
	},
	{
		axis: 'Source you can version and review',
		why: 'Diff two drafts, review a change, revert a bad edit.',
		marks: ['yes', 'no', 'no', 'yes', 'no'],
	},
	{
		axis: 'Brand enforced as tokens, not promises',
		why: 'The theme owns the pixels; nobody can drift off-brand.',
		marks: ['yes', 'part', 'part', 'part', 'part'],
	},
	{
		axis: 'Boardroom polish with no design work',
		why: 'Looks finished before you touch it.',
		marks: ['yes', 'part', 'part', 'no', 'yes'],
	},
	{
		axis: 'Export fidelity across formats',
		why: 'It looks the same on every machine you send it to.',
		marks: ['yes', 'no', 'part', 'part', 'part'],
	},
	{
		axis: 'Real-time co-editing',
		why: 'Several people in one live document.',
		marks: ['no', 'yes', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'],
	},
	{
		axis: 'WYSIWYG, no syntax to learn',
		why: 'Drag, drop, done — nothing to memorize.',
		marks: ['no', 'yes', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'],
	},
	{
		axis: 'Reliably agent / AI-authorable',
		why: 'An LLM drives it to on-brand output, not a guess.',
		marks: ['yes', 'part', 'part', 'part', 'part'],
	},
	{
		axis: 'No lock-in (your file, offline, open source)',
		why: 'It runs where you run it and can never be switched off.',
		marks: ['yes', 'no', 'no', 'yes', 'no'],
	},
];
const MARK = {
	yes: { glyph: '●', label: 'Yes', cls: 'mk-yes' },
	part: { glyph: '◐', label: 'Partial', cls: 'mk-part' },
	no: { glyph: '○', label: 'No', cls: 'mk-no' },
};

// ── The receipts (sourced evidence, refreshed July 2026) ─────────────────────
// Every card links out and carries the caveat a skeptical reader should want.
const RECEIPTS = [
	{
		stat: '5 of 45',
		head: 'Citations correct in a Big Four firm’s own AI report',
		body: 'KPMG’s 2025 report on agentic AI cited 45 sources. Five were correct. The rest were fabricated or mangled by AI — invented authors, wrong dates, papers that don’t exist. A firm that audits others couldn’t audit its own deck. KPMG pulled the report.',
		cite: 'GPTZero investigation, 2026',
		href: 'https://gptzero.me/news/investigations-kpmg/',
		note: 'GPTZero’s primary analysis, corroborated by The Register and the OECD AI Incidents monitor. The sharpest evidence that a fabricated fact ships at the speed of a draft.',
	},
	{
		stat: 'AU$440k',
		head: 'A government report that invented its sources',
		body: 'Deloitte produced a AU$440k report for the Australian government that cited non-existent papers and a fabricated quote attributed to a Federal Court judge. Deloitte refunded the contract’s final installment and reissued a version disclosing it had used GPT-4o.',
		cite: 'Fortune / The Register, Oct 2025',
		href: 'https://www.theregister.com/2025/10/06/deloitte_ai_report_australia/',
		note: 'Verified across Fortune, The Register, and CFO Dive. The AU$440k is the contract value; the refund was the final installment, not the whole fee.',
	},
	{
		stat: '0–20%',
		head: 'How much of an AI deck checks out',
		body: 'One team fed the same 10-slide prompt to six presentation tools and verified every claim against primary sources. Gamma scored 20%. Beautiful.ai and Canva managed 17%. Tome hit 0%. Canva pinned invented statistics to a real, named school. Speed to a draft is not speed to something true.',
		cite: 'Fact-check of 6 AI presentation makers, Feb 2026',
		href: 'https://medium.com/@neel2108/we-fact-checked-6-ai-presentation-makers-heres-how-often-they-hallucinate-90e6093e42fc',
		note: 'One author, one prompt per tool — read it as directional, not a large study. The mechanism holds: an LLM predicts plausible text; it does not look facts up.',
	},
	{
		stat: '25M',
		head: 'Users whose decks got switched off',
		body: 'Tome reached 25 million users, then shut its presentation product down in 2025 — “we killed a product used by 25M people,” its co-founder wrote — because almost nobody would pay for the output. Decks nobody exported in time were gone. A Markdown deck in your own folder cannot be switched off under you.',
		cite: 'Founder’s post + VentureBeat, 2025',
		href: 'https://venturebeat.com/technology/tomes-founders-ditch-viral-presentation-app-with-20m-users-to-build-ai',
		note: 'Under 2% of users paid, on under ~$4M revenue against $81M raised. Reach is not the same as value — or permanence.',
	},
];

// ── Lattice's honest weaknesses ─────────────────────────────────────────────
const WEAKNESSES = [
	{
		head: 'No WYSIWYG canvas',
		body: 'You write Markdown and pick a component; you don’t drag a box across a canvas. The Drawing Board shows a live preview, not direct manipulation. If you think in pictures instead of structure, that grates.',
	},
	{
		head: 'A real learning curve',
		body: 'Markdown, a vocabulary of layouts, and an authoring contract with rules. The tooling teaches as you go — autocomplete, a live gallery, an inline linter — but your first deck still takes longer than typing a prompt into Gamma.',
	},
	{
		head: 'No real-time co-editing',
		body: 'Collaboration is asynchronous: you review a change and merge it, rather than sharing five live cursors in one document. That’s how code and serious documents are edited — but if your team lives in Google Slides, it’s a real change of habit.',
	},
	{
		head: 'Setup, not sign-up',
		body: 'To render locally you install a toolchain; it runs offline and phones home to nobody. The playground needs none of that — but clone-and-install still asks more of you than opening a browser tab, and the desktop app that removes the last of it hasn’t shipped.',
	},
	{
		head: 'A young ecosystem',
		body: 'The engine is at v1 and the components and tokens are stable, but the tooling around it is young and the desktop app is still in development. You’re early.',
	},
	{
		head: 'Not for the stage',
		body: 'No builds, no transitions, no presenter animations. Lattice makes documents you email and archive and that read without you in the room. For a live keynote with stagecraft, use Keynote.',
	},
];

// ── Where Lattice is going ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
const ROADMAP = [
	{
		head: 'SlideWright desktop',
		body: 'A native app wraps the same engine, for people who want the determinism and skip the terminal entirely.',
		accent: false,
	},
	{
		head: 'Deeper agent authoring',
		body: 'The machine catalog and the shared linter make Lattice an easy target for an LLM — so an agent drafts a deck that lands on brand instead of guessing at layout.',
		accent: false,
	},
	{
		head: 'One we’re not ready to name',
		body: 'One more capability is coming. It changes what a deterministic deck engine can do. We’ll ship it when it’s ready, on the same engine, with the same guarantees.',
		accent: true,
	},
];
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" data-palette="indaco" data-mode="light">
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8" />
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
		<ResourceHints />
		<title>The case for a deterministic deck — Lattice vs. the alternatives</title>
		<meta
			name="description"
			content="Why a serious deck shouldn’t be a gamble, and making one shouldn’t need a designer — the problem, what Lattice believes, what it commits to, and an honest, sourced comparison against AI generators, Office+AI, code engines, and design tools, including where Lattice is the wrong call."
		/>
		<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href={url('favicon.svg')} />
		<script is:inline>
			(function () {
				var r = document.documentElement;
				try {
					var p = localStorage.getItem('lattice-docs-palette');
					if (p) r.setAttribute('data-palette', p);
					var m = localStorage.getItem('lattice-docs-mode');
					if (m === 'light' || m === 'dark') r.setAttribute('data-mode', m);
					else if (window.matchMedia && matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches)
						r.setAttribute('data-mode', 'dark');
				} catch (e) {}
			})();
		</script>
		<style>
			/* Comparison-matrix marks — color-blind-safe glyphs + tokenized hues. */
			.mk { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-size: 18px; line-height: 1; }
			.mk-yes { color: var(--accent); }
			.mk-part { color: var(--text-muted); }
			.mk-no { color: var(--border); }
			.cmp-table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; min-width: 720px; }
			.cmp-table th, .cmp-table td { padding: 13px 14px; text-align: center; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
			.cmp-table thead th { font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-heading); vertical-align: bottom; }
			.cmp-table thead th.col-lattice { color: var(--accent); }
			.cmp-table td.axis, .cmp-table th.axis { text-align: left; width: 38%; }
			.cmp-table tbody tr:hover { background: var(--bg-alt); }
			.cmp-table .col-lattice { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 6%, transparent); }
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<SiteHeader palettes={palettes} />

		<main>
			<!-- Hero — lead with the belief, not their speed -->
			<section class="py-[clamp(48px,7vw,96px)] pb-[clamp(32px,4vw,56px)]">
				<div class="wrap max-w-[54rem]">
					<p class="m-0 mb-4 font-mono text-[12px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-primary">
						The case for a deterministic deck
					</p>
					<h1 class="m-0 mb-5 font-[var(--font-display)] text-[clamp(34px,5.2vw,60px)] font-bold leading-[1.04] tracking-[-0.02em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">
						A serious deck shouldn’t be a gamble. Making one shouldn’t need a designer.
					</h1>
					<p class="m-0 mb-4 text-[clamp(17px,2vw,20px)] leading-[1.5] text-foreground">
						Every other tool makes you choose — fast or trustworthy, easy or on brand, beautiful or yours to keep.
						Lattice is the argument that you shouldn’t have to. A deck is plain text; a design system does the design;
						and the render you approve is the render that opens in the room.
					</p>
					<p class="m-0 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.6] text-muted-foreground">
						This page makes that case in full: the problem, what we believe a deck should be, what we commit to — and
						an honest, sourced read on every alternative, including where Lattice is the wrong call.
					</p>
					<div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
						<a
							href={url('playground/')}
							class="inline-flex items-center rounded-lg bg-primary px-5 py-2.5 text-[15px] font-semibold shadow-sm transition-all hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:shadow-lg"
							style="color: var(--on-accent);"
						>
							Try the playground
						</a>
						<a
							href="#matrix"
							class="inline-flex items-center rounded-lg border border-border bg-card px-5 py-2.5 text-[15px] font-semibold transition-colors hover:border-primary"
							style="color: var(--text-body);"
						>
							Jump to the comparison
						</a>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- The problem -->
			<section class="border-t border-border py-[clamp(48px,6vw,88px)]">
				<div class="wrap">
					<SectionHead
						eyebrow="The problem"
						title="Three decades of tools copied PowerPoint’s blank canvas — and its blank check."
					>
						A slide tool hands you an empty rectangle and no guardrails. Nothing stops the drift, enforces the brand,
						or catches the contrast that fails and the chart that’s unreadable in gray. The burden of consistency falls
						on you, slide after slide, the night before the meeting — and you can’t even tell what changed between two
						versions. So we all sit through decks that are worse than they needed to be, not because the author had
						nothing to say, but because the tool fought them the whole way.
					</SectionHead>
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-[18px] lg:grid-cols-[1.15fr_1fr]">
						<div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
							<h3 class="m-0 mb-3 font-[var(--font-body)] text-[19px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">
								AI didn’t fix the foundation. It automated the drift.
							</h3>
							<p class="m-0 mb-3 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								Bolting a model onto the blank canvas made the first draft faster and the finish worse. The output has
								a name now — “slop,” Merriam-Webster’s word of 2025 — the same gradient, the same font, the same
								rounded card, on every deck. Sameness is the tax you pay for design-by-generation.
							</p>
							<p class="m-0 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								The foundation was wrong for today’s work, and no amount of AI layered on top can mask a flaw that deep.
								The fix isn’t a smarter canvas. It’s no canvas at all.
							</p>
						</div>
						<blockquote class="m-0 flex flex-col justify-center rounded-xl border border-border bg-muted p-[26px]">
							<p class="m-0 mb-2.5 text-[17px] italic leading-[1.5] text-[var(--text-heading)]">
								“Consistency is the principle that separates professional design from amateur work. When consistency
								breaks, so does the reader’s trust.”
							</p>
							<footer class="text-[13px] text-muted-foreground">— on the typography of professional services</footer>
						</blockquote>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- What we believe -->
			<section class="border-y border-border bg-muted py-[clamp(48px,6vw,88px)]">
				<div class="wrap">
					<SectionHead
						eyebrow="What we believe"
						title="The system carries the consistency. You carry the judgment."
					>
						Most of what goes wrong in a deck is mechanical — contrast, spacing, alignment, a color that’s the only
						signal a color-blind reader can’t see. None of it is a matter of judgment, and none of it should cost you
						any. Lattice clears all of it off your desk automatically, so the only thing left in front of you is the
						thinking: whether the claim is true, whether the slide earns its place, whether the argument lands. The
						machine owns what’s correct; you own what’s good.
					</SectionHead>
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-[18px] lg:grid-cols-3">
						<div class="flex flex-col rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
							<h3 class="m-0 mb-3 text-[18px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">Good design, made a default</h3>
							<p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								What reads as authoritative isn’t more design — it’s disciplined consistency and restraint. A design
								system makes that automatic, which means presence stops being a skill you need and becomes a default
								you get. You don’t have to be a designer.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="flex flex-col rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
							<h3 class="m-0 mb-3 text-[18px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">A slide is four decisions</h3>
							<p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								Function, Form, Substance, Finish — what the slide is for, the shape that holds it, what fills the
								shape, and the palette and mood. You make them one at a time instead of fighting all four at once on a
								blank canvas. Change one without disturbing the rest.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="flex flex-col rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
							<h3 class="m-0 mb-3 text-[18px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">Built to be read without you</h3>
							<p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								A board deck gets forwarded, attached, opened in a room you were never in. It has to carry its point
								alone — one clear claim, a heading that says something, a shape that fits the thought. Lattice is built
								for the deck that still makes the case after you’ve left.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- On speed — the reframe -->
			<section class="py-[clamp(48px,6vw,88px)]">
				<div class="wrap">
					<SectionHead
						eyebrow="On speed"
						title="Fast to a draft is not fast to a deck."
					>
						An AI generator is genuinely fast — to a first draft. That’s the well-documented 80/20 of generative AI:
						the demo lands in a minute, and the last mile is still yours. Then the real clock starts, and it runs long.
					</SectionHead>
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-[18px] lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1fr]">
						<div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
							<h3 class="m-0 mb-3 text-[18px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">Their clock</h3>
							<p class="m-0 text-[15px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								You fact-check the numbers the model invented. You strip the sameness so it doesn’t read as a template.
								You fix the export that swapped your fonts for Calibri. You force the whole thing back onto the brand.
								The draft arrived in a minute; the deck you can actually send is hours away — and you feel faster than
								you are the entire time.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="rounded-xl border border-primary p-[26px] shadow-sm" style="background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 7%, transparent);">
							<h3 class="m-0 mb-3 text-[18px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-primary">Ours</h3>
							<p class="m-0 text-[15px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								Lattice front-loads none of that. The design decisions were made before you arrived; the render you
								approve is the render you send — no re-brand, no broken PPTX, no fact-checking the layout. The first
								render is the final render. That’s the speed that matters: not speed to a draft, speed to something you
								can put your name on.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
					<p class="mt-8 max-w-[46ch] font-[var(--font-display)] text-[clamp(22px,2.6vw,30px)] font-bold leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">
						The artsy deck wins the demo. The deterministic deck wins the boardroom.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- What we commit to (policy) -->
			<section class="border-y border-border bg-muted py-[clamp(48px,6vw,88px)]">
				<div class="wrap">
					<SectionHead
						eyebrow="What we commit to"
						title="Four promises the render path keeps."
					>
						These aren’t features on a list; they’re the guarantees the whole engine is built to hold. Everything else
						on this page follows from them.
					</SectionHead>
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-[18px] sm:grid-cols-2">
						{COMMITMENTS.map((c, i) => (
							<div class="flex gap-4 rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
								<div class="flex-none font-[var(--font-display)] text-[24px] font-bold leading-none text-primary">
									{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
								</div>
								<div>
									<h3 class="m-0 mb-2 text-[18px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">{c.head}</h3>
									<p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">{c.body}</p>
								</div>
							</div>
						))}
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Now, the field — the honest comparison -->
			<section class="py-[clamp(48px,6vw,88px)]">
				<div class="wrap">
					<SectionHead
						eyebrow="Now, the field"
						title="An honest read on every alternative — including where each one wins."
					>
						That’s the argument. Here’s the map. We credit each family’s real strengths and name where Lattice is the
						wrong call, because a comparison you can’t trust is worth nothing. The presentation world splits into four
						camps.
					</SectionHead>
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-[18px] lg:grid-cols-2">
						{FAMILIES.map((f) => (
							<div class="flex flex-col rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
								<h3 class="m-0 mb-1 font-[var(--font-body)] text-[20px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">
									{f.name}
								</h3>
								<p class="m-0 mb-4 font-mono text-[12.5px] leading-[1.5] text-muted-foreground">{f.tools}</p>
								<p class="m-0 mb-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
									<span class="font-semibold text-primary">Where it wins. </span>{f.win}
								</p>
								<p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
									<span class="font-semibold text-[var(--text-heading)]">Where it falls short. </span>{f.fall}
								</p>
							</div>
						))}
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- The matrix -->
			<section id="matrix" class="scroll-mt-20 border-y border-border bg-muted py-[clamp(48px,6vw,88px)]">
				<div class="wrap">
					<SectionHead
						eyebrow="Side by side"
						title="The capabilities that decide a high-stakes deck."
					>
						We score capability, not feature count. Lattice loses rows here. It trades live collaboration and
						point-and-click ease for reproducible output, enforced brand, and a file you own. That trade is the whole
						pitch — and it’s the right one exactly when the deck matters.
					</SectionHead>
					<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card">
						<table class="cmp-table">
							<thead>
								<tr>
									<th class="axis">Capability</th>
									{COLS.map((c) => (
										<th class={c === 'Lattice' ? 'col-lattice' : undefined}>{c}</th>
									))}
								</tr>
							</thead>
							<tbody>
								{ROWS.map((r) => (
									<tr>
										<td class="axis">
											<span class="block text-[14.5px] font-semibold text-[var(--text-heading)]">{r.axis}</span>
											<span class="block text-[12.5px] leading-[1.45] text-muted-foreground">{r.why}</span>
										</td>
										{r.marks.map((m, i) => (
											<td class={COLS[i] === 'Lattice' ? 'col-lattice' : undefined}>
												<span class={`mk ${MARK[m].cls}`} role="img" aria-label={MARK[m].label}>{MARK[m].glyph}</span>
											</td>
										))}
									</tr>
								))}
							</tbody>
						</table>
					</div>
					<p class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-5 gap-y-1.5 text-[13px] text-muted-foreground">
						<span><span class="mk mk-yes align-middle">●</span> Strong</span>
						<span><span class="mk mk-part align-middle">◐</span> Partial / conditional</span>
						<span><span class="mk mk-no align-middle">○</span> No</span>
						<span class="italic">Columns are families; individual tools vary — see the notes above and the sources below.</span>
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- The receipts -->
			<section class="py-[clamp(48px,6vw,88px)]">
				<div class="wrap">
					<SectionHead
						eyebrow="The receipts"
						title="The case, with sources attached."
					>
						Strong claims need evidence. Every claim below links out and carries the caveat a skeptical reader should
						want — including where the source is thin.
					</SectionHead>
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-[18px] sm:grid-cols-2">
						{RECEIPTS.map((r) => (
							<div class="flex flex-col rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
								<div class="mb-3 font-[var(--font-display)] text-[clamp(30px,3.6vw,42px)] font-bold leading-none text-primary">
									{r.stat}
								</div>
								<h3 class="m-0 mb-2.5 font-[var(--font-body)] text-[17px] font-semibold leading-[1.3] text-[var(--text-heading)]">
									{r.head}
								</h3>
								<p class="m-0 mb-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">{r.body}</p>
								<p class="m-0 mb-3 text-[13px] italic leading-[1.5] text-muted-foreground">{r.note}</p>
								<a
									href={r.href}
									class="mt-auto inline-flex w-fit text-[13px] font-semibold text-primary hover:underline"
									rel="nofollow noopener"
									target="_blank"
								>
									{r.cite} ↗
								</a>
							</div>
						))}
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Isn't this just Marp? -->
			<section class="border-y border-border bg-muted py-[clamp(48px,6vw,88px)]">
				<div class="wrap">
					<SectionHead
						eyebrow="The fair question"
						title="“Isn’t Lattice just Marp with a coat of paint?”"
					>
						No. Lattice runs on its own engine and its own design system. Marp is one export path out of a
						Lattice deck; the engine inside is ours.
					</SectionHead>
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-[18px] lg:grid-cols-2">
						<div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
							<h3 class="m-0 mb-3 text-[17px] font-semibold text-[var(--text-heading)]">What we started from</h3>
							<p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.65] text-foreground">
								One good idea: write a deck in Markdown and render it through headless Chromium to PDF. Plain text
								buys determinism and reviewable diffs, true of every code engine. We built on Marp’s engine for the
								early versions, then replaced it with our own at v1.0.0.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
							<h3 class="m-0 mb-3 text-[17px] font-semibold text-[var(--text-heading)]">What Lattice is now</h3>
							<p class="m-0 mb-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.65] text-foreground">
								Marp’s own docs say it has <strong>“no component system, design tokens, slots, or layout
								primitives,”</strong> and it ships three generic themes. Lattice brings all of it:
							</p>
							<ul class="m-0 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.55] text-foreground">
								<li><strong>More than fifty components</strong> across thirteen buckets. Pick the meaning, get the composition.</li>
								<li>A <strong>design-token system</strong> and <strong>14 editorial palettes + 4 color-blind-safe</strong>. Layouts never name a color.</li>
								<li><strong>Boardroom-grade defaults</strong> and WCAG-AA contrast in every palette, light and dark.</li>
								<li>An <strong>owned, pinned render engine</strong>. We dropped the Marp dependency at v1.0.0 and pinned the toolchain end to end. It renders plain HTML sections, not SVG-wrapped slides, so modern CSS — container queries, counters, masks — works natively and layout holds across browsers.</li>
								<li><strong>Agent authoring.</strong> A machine catalog and a shared linter steer an LLM to on-brand decks instead of guesses.</li>
							</ul>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- On AI: bring your own model -->
			<section class="border-t border-border py-[clamp(48px,6vw,88px)]">
				<div class="wrap">
					<SectionHead
						eyebrow="On AI"
						title="Bring your own model. We don’t own the AI layer."
					>
						The AI generators own the model. You get theirs, priced their way, swapped out from under you whenever
						they like. Lattice takes the opposite stance: the AI layer is yours. You pick the model today, and you keep
						that choice tomorrow.
					</SectionHead>
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-[18px] lg:grid-cols-3">
						<div class="flex flex-col rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
							<h3 class="m-0 mb-3 font-[var(--font-body)] text-[18px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">
								Your model, your choice
							</h3>
							<p class="m-0 mb-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								Lattice talks to <strong>OpenRouter</strong>, so you can drive any of 500+ models — Claude, GPT,
								Gemini, DeepSeek, Llama, and the free tiers — and switch whenever you want.
							</p>
							<p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								We build and test Lattice on <strong>Claude</strong>, because we think it’s the best, and we default to
								<strong>Sonnet</strong> for the best work per dollar. It’s a default, not a cage.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="flex flex-col rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
							<h3 class="m-0 mb-3 font-[var(--font-body)] text-[18px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">
								Built to keep the bill down
							</h3>
							<p class="m-0 mb-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								We use AI too, and we know how fast the meter runs. So the Coach finds and fixes most issues with
								<strong>no model call at all</strong> — you spend tokens only when you actually ask the AI to write
								something.
							</p>
							<p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								When it does call out, Lattice <strong>caches the instructions</strong> across your whole session and
								sends the model a <strong>compact catalog</strong> instead of a wall of docs. Less in, less out, lower
								bill.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="flex flex-col rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
							<h3 class="m-0 mb-3 font-[var(--font-body)] text-[18px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">
								You hold the controls
							</h3>
							<p class="m-0 mb-3 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								OpenRouter handles billing and routing; Lattice adds a layer on top. Pick a model with its
								<strong>live price</strong> in front of you. Set a <strong>session budget</strong> — Lattice warns at
								80% and can stop at 100%.
							</p>
							<p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								Your spend and your account balance stay on screen the whole time. No surprise invoice at the end of
								the month.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
					<p class="mt-6 max-w-[68ch] text-[14px] leading-[1.6] text-muted-foreground">
						Today you connect your own OpenRouter account in one click — your key, your credits, your spend.
						<span class="font-semibold text-[var(--text-heading)]">Next:</span> bring your own provider key, so you can
						point Lattice straight at Anthropic or OpenAI and cut out the middle layer entirely.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Our warts -->
			<section class="py-[clamp(48px,6vw,88px)]">
				<div class="wrap">
					<SectionHead
						eyebrow="Where we’re weaker"
						title="When Lattice is the wrong tool."
					>
						An honest page owns its weak spots. If any of these is a hard requirement, use something else. We’d rather
						lose you here than disappoint you later.
					</SectionHead>
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-[18px] sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
						{WEAKNESSES.map((w) => (
							<div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[24px] shadow-sm">
								<h3 class="m-0 mb-2 text-[16px] font-semibold text-[var(--text-heading)]">{w.head}</h3>
								<p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">{w.body}</p>
							</div>
						))}
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Where Lattice is going -->
			<section class="border-y border-border bg-muted py-[clamp(48px,6vw,88px)]">
				<div class="wrap">
					<SectionHead
						eyebrow="Where Lattice is going"
						title="The deterministic core is the floor, not the ceiling."
					>
						Everything above ships today at v1. Every roadmap item runs on the same engine, with the same guarantees,
						and adds reach without giving any of it up.
					</SectionHead>
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-[18px] sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
						{ROADMAP.map((r) => (
							r.accent ? (
								<div class="rounded-xl border border-primary p-[24px] shadow-sm" style="background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 7%, transparent);">
									<h3 class="m-0 mb-2 text-[16px] font-semibold text-primary">{r.head}</h3>
									<p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">{r.body}</p>
								</div>
							) : (
								<div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[24px] shadow-sm">
									<h3 class="m-0 mb-2 text-[16px] font-semibold text-[var(--text-heading)]">{r.head}</h3>
									<p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">{r.body}</p>
								</div>
							)
						))}
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- CTA -->
			<section class="py-[clamp(48px,6vw,88px)]">
				<div class="wrap">
					<SectionHead eyebrow="See for yourself" title="Don’t take our word for it." />
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-[18px] sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
						<a
							href={url('playground/')}
							class="group block rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] text-card-foreground shadow-sm transition-all hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-primary hover:shadow-lg"
						>
							<h3 class="mb-2 font-[var(--font-body)] text-[19px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">Open the playground</h3>
							<p class="m-0 text-[15px] text-foreground">Write a slide, switch every palette, export a real PDF. In the browser, right now.</p>
						</a>
						<a
							href={url('getting-started/')}
							class="group block rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] text-card-foreground shadow-sm transition-all hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-primary hover:shadow-lg"
						>
							<h3 class="mb-2 font-[var(--font-body)] text-[19px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">Render your first deck</h3>
							<p class="m-0 text-[15px] text-foreground">Install the toolchain and produce a boardroom-grade PDF in a few minutes.</p>
						</a>
						<a
							href={SOURCE_DOC}
							class="group block rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] text-card-foreground shadow-sm transition-all hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-primary hover:shadow-lg"
							rel="nofollow noopener"
							target="_blank"
						>
							<h3 class="mb-2 font-[var(--font-body)] text-[19px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">Read the method ↗</h3>
							<p class="m-0 text-[15px] text-foreground">Every claim on this page, with its sources, its caveats, and the parts we could not fully verify.</p>
						</a>
					</div>
					<p class="mt-8 max-w-[62ch] text-[13px] leading-[1.6] text-muted-foreground">
						Method: we surveyed the field in 2026 and lean on primary sources first — official docs and pricing pages,
						named studies, first-party postmortems — then recent reviews. These tools move fast and some claims are
						contested, pricing and AI features most of all. Where we could not verify a number, we kept it qualitative.
						Spot something out of date? Send a correction.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>
		</main>

		<footer class="foot">
			<div class="wrap foot-inner">
				<a class="brand" href={url('')}>
					<img class="brand-logo" src={url('lattice-mark-min.svg')} alt="" width="26" height="26" />
					<span class="brand-mark">Lattice</span>
				</a>
				<nav>
					<a href={url('introduction/')}>What is Lattice?</a>
					<a href={url('story/')}>Story</a>
					<a href={url('getting-started/')}>Get started</a>
					<a href={url('playground/')}>Playground</a>
					<a href={url('drawing-board/')}>Drawing Board</a>
					<a href={url('workbench/')}>Workbench</a>
					<a href={url('guides/authoring/')}>Authoring</a>
					<a href={url('components/')}>Components</a>
					<a href={url('comparison/')}>Comparison</a>
